<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Package sources for this solution. <clear /> drops any inherited sources from
    machine-level NuGet.Config so the build is reproducible from this file alone.
    Directory.Build.props turns on RestorePackagesWithLockFile, and without this
    file the committed packages.lock.json files would resolve against whatever
    machine-level configuration happened to be present rather than against a
    stated set of sources.

    Deliberately no `github` source and no packageSourceCredentials block, as of
    migration step 7 (halheinrich/Math#10). This repository consumes no
    HalHeinrich.* package: its BigRational edge is a ProjectReference, not a
    PackageReference, so the GITHUB_PACKAGES_PAT this file used to require would
    be a restore failure mode with nothing behind it. Dead credential config is
    worse than dead config - it implies a secret is needed when none is. Only
    ContinuedFractions.Tests restores from a feed at all: the xUnit, test-SDK and
    coverage tooling, all of it public. ContinuedFractions itself carries no
    PackageReference. Add the source and the credentials back only if a
    HalHeinrich.* package is ever referenced from this repo again.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Deliberately no packageSourceMapping. Until step 7 this file held the one
    correct instance of it in the umbrella - it declared the github source *and*
    mapped HalHeinrich.* to it, which is what made the dependency-confusion
    defence real, where the members that copied the comment without the source
    had halheinrich/Math#30's inert construct. With the source gone the mapping
    has nothing left to defend, and what would remain is that same inert
    construct.

    With one declared source a `*` mapping to nuget.org is inert for every
    package this repo restores: it selects exactly the resolution no mapping at
    all produces. Its only effect would arrive on the day a second source is
    added, and measured on SDK 10.0.400 that effect points the wrong way. With a
    mapping present NuGet considers only the sources whose patterns match a given
    package id and names the rest as "not considered" in the restore error - so a
    `*` mapped to nuget.org would exclude a newly added github feed from every
    package, the HalHeinrich.* one that motivated adding the feed included. The
    private name would then be sought on the public feed and nowhere else, which
    is the direction dependency confusion runs rather than a defence against it.

    The defence that does work cannot be written in advance, because it has to
    name the source it protects: when a HalHeinrich.* PackageReference is added,
    add the github source above and, in that same change, a packageSourceMapping
    section mapping `HalHeinrich.*` to github and `*` to nuget.org.
  -->

</configuration>
